    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Some believe Jennings was named for frontier lawyer turned outlaw Alphonzo Jennings, who donated forty acres for the townsite.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=JE005"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "By 1905, Jennings boasted ten stone buildings, two broom factories, and a quiet and orderly saloon.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=JE005"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Missouri, Kansas and Oklahoma Railroad connected Jennings with outside markets by laying track from Agra to Osage.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
